.TH GRPALETTETEST 1
.SH NAME
grpalettetest \- check the TempleOS colour registers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grpalettetest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Grpalettetest
checks
.IR grpalette (2):
that
.B EGA
is still the sixteen colours the
.IR temple (1)
ports had written into them one file at a time, that
.B RICH
did not quietly change what an index means, that a register behaves like a
register, and that the true-colour arithmetic is right at its endpoints.
It needs no display and runs under
.BR emu-g .
.PP
The EGA table it compares against is an independent copy of the literal
that stood in the ports.
It is deliberately not read from the module: a test that asks the module
for the answer and then compares it with the module's answer checks
nothing.
.PP
.B RICH
is not checked against a table \(em it is allowed to look different \(em
but against the properties that make it the same palette.
.B BLACK
and
.B WHITE
must be exact, every entry must be opaque, no channel ordering that
.B EGA
states may be reversed, index
.I i
must stay darker than index
.IB i +8,
and the palette must differ from
.B EGA
somewhere, so that a
.B RICH
that had silently become a copy of
.B EGA
would be caught.
.PP
.B -v
names each check as it passes.
The exit status is an error if any check fails.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/grpalettetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR grpalette (2),
.IR temple (1)
.SH BUGS
Nothing here draws.
.B images
and
.B ramp
allocate against a
.BR Draw->Display ,
so they are not exercised at all, and neither is the
.B $grpalette
knob in any setting but the one the test happens to run under \(em it
checks that the loaded palette agrees with the environment, which is
vacuous when the variable is unset unless the test is run again with it
set.
No check compares a rendered frame: that a port looks right with a palette
loaded is not established here.
